\par\par \b \uc2  Thai Baht/Ends -2: Interbank Rates Steady, Swaps Down \b0\par\par\uc2 324 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Oktober 1998\par 14:00\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Thailand's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Deputy Governor Kitti Patpongpibul told reporters Thursday the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  is prepared to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets if the baht is too volatile. \par \par \uc2  Kitti said the baht's strength was a sign of investor confidence, and would help the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  keep domestic interest rates low. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Thailand set its reference rate at THB38.841 to the dollar Thursday, compared with THB38.882 Wednesday. The reference rate is a weighted average of rates charged by Thai commercial banks the previous trading day. It serves as a benchmark for domestic \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading. \par \par \uc2  Domestic money-market rates were steady Thursday. Overnight and call rates were around 4%-5%, unchanged for two weeks. The rates were at 9%-10% in mid-August. \par \par \uc2  The overnight repurchase rate ended at 5.4375%, down from 5.50% at the close Wednesday. The one-month rate was unchanged at 7.50%. \par \par \uc2  The repurchase market is a lending market between the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and local financial institutions, where government securities are placed as collateral. \par \par \uc2  Repurchase market rates were near 20% in mid-June, prior to an easing in monetary policy. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Offshore swap premiums were lower Thursday as follows, in satang\par (THB1=100 satang):\par        Thursday     Wednesday     Tuesday      Monday\par 1-mo    4.0-6.0      5.0-7.0