\par\par \b \uc2  SE Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2 -3: Baht Run-Up May Be Nearing Peak \b0\par\par\uc2 310 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 September 1998\par 12:25\par \uc2  Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  U.S. dollar sales also pushed the Thai baht higher from Friday's Asian close, with investment inflows contributing to the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rise, according to traders. But given the speed of the baht's recent surge, which saw the Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rise by around 3.7% over the course of the last week, some traders ventured that the Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  may be nearing the end of its recent bull run. \par \par \uc2  Despite the Bank of Thailand's assertions last week that it is prepared to let market forces determine the baht's exchange rate, many traders still believe that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stands ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Should rapid appreciation of the baht threaten \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2 's export competitiveness, the Bank of Thailand is likely to step into the market, buying dollars to hold the baht down, they say. \par \par \uc2  "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will try to temper any drastic moves," said the U.S. bank trading chief, adding that the authorities are likely to act to prevent the U.S. dollar falling below the 38.00 baht (THB) level. \par \par \uc2  Late in Asian trading, the U.S. dollar was quoted at THB39.1250, down from THB39.4500 on Friday. \par \par \uc2  Among other regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the Indonesian rupiah ended little changed, with the U.S. dollar closing Asian trading at IDR10,913, compared with IDR10,900 on Friday. Interest in the market was minimal, according to dealers. \par \par \uc2  The Philippine peso, however, slipped steeply against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as domestic corporations took the opportunity to buy up cheap dollars, following the peso's rise on Friday. At the close of trading on the Philippine Dealing System, the U.S. dollar was at 43.94 pesos (PHP), up from 43.76 at Friday's close. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) Dow Jones Newswires  09-28-98 \par \par \uc2  0625EDT \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020010916du9s029cc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
