\par\par \b \uc2  ASIA MARKETS - Stocks jumpy, central banks active. \b0\par\par\uc2 409 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 September 1998\par 12:03\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Sept 22 (Reuters) - Major Asian stock markets cheered up on Tuesday after Wall Street shrugged off the global airing of U.S. President Bill Clinton's grand jury testimony. But traders said Asia's markets were still jumpy, especially over the implications of Japan's protracted financial crisis. Regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were hurt by yen weakness, and some saw \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  HONG KONG STOCKS CLIMB WHILE MANILA SINKS \par \par \uc2  Hong Kong shares saw big early gains on thin volume, prompted by Wall Street's positive response to the political developments surrounding Clinton's testimony. \par \par \uc2  Bell-wether stocks HSBC Holdings, Hong Kong Telecom, China Telecom and Hutchison Whampoa were heavily traded. \par \par \uc2  But dealers declined to attribute the buying to genuine interest and said sellers were "backing off". \par \par \uc2  Philippine shares were the biggest losers on Tuesday, closing 3.5 percent lower as the reality of economic malaise hit home. \par \par \uc2  The country is coming to grips with an increasingly bleak economic outlook, economists said. \par \par \uc2  The stock market was hurt by confusion over leading blue chips Philippine Long Distance Telephone, rumoured to be a foreign takeover target, and Philippine Airlines (PAL), the national airline which is set to formally close on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Most market watchers felt PAL would not be forced into liquidation and was engaged in brinkmanship with the government, which has shown reluctance to bail out the airline. \par \par \uc2  CENTRAL BANKS IN THE \b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  MARKET \par \par \uc2  Limited \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  activity was seen in some \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, with fears of yen-induced falls pushing a few of the regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  through what were seen as key levels. \par \par \uc2  The yen was quoted around 135 to the dollar at 0950 GMT. \par \par \uc2  Korean \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities were seen in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, after the won threatened to fall through 1,400 to the dollar on the back of the weak yen. \par \par \uc2  Speculative positions built up early on the dollar's recovery sparked verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of Korea, dealers said. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  appeared to be looking for dollars on Tuesday as the baht weakened against the greenback, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The government is still cleaning up remaining long baht positions taken in the forward market by the Bank of Thailand in the past year, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar was supported by firmer short-term interest rates, but some said the rise was odd given the twin political concerns of Malaysia and Indonesia. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021108du9m01nr0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
