\par\par \b \uc2  TEXT-Thailand letter of intent with IMF. \b0\par\par\uc2 6,505 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 August 1998\par 15:47\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  WASHINGTON, Aug 26 (Reuters) - The following is the full text of Thailand's letter of \par \par \uc2  intent outlining its programme with the International Monetary Fund. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab    Bangkok\par \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab    August 25, 1998\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Dear Mr. Camdessus:\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 1. We have continued to make considerable progress in implementing the\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par programme, and all performance criteria have been met. This has helped\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par consolidate Thailand's external situation, increase further net\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par international reserves, and maintain broad stability in the baht. These are\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par significant achievements given the deepening of the recession in Asia.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 2. However, Thailand has been deeply affected by the regional crisis, and\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par domestic output has fallen further. Although an export recovery is\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par emerging, with volumes having increased by about 10 percent during the\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par first half of 1998, there are severe constraints from the decline in\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par commodity prices and depressed regional demand. Domestically, consumer and\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par investor confidence has been slow to recover, and unemployment is rising.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 3. Against this background, we are resolved to intensify restructuring\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par efforts in crucial sectors of the economy, and further adapt the program's\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par policy framework. These modifications are designed to be as supportive as\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par possible of an early recovery, and as protective as possible of social\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par conditions. At this fourth quarterly review of the programme, the focus of\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par policies will therefore be in the following six areas:\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    * First, within the macroeconomic framework, fiscal policy will continue\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     to aim at supporting domestic demand. Therefore, the overall public\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     sector deficit for 1998/99 is targeted to remain at about 3 percent of\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     GDP. This excludes 1` percent of GDP in interest costs related to the\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     ongoing fiscalization of bonds for FIDF and other financial\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     restructuring costs. Of these costs, 1 percent had already been\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     budgeted; an additional ` percent is earmarked for the initial\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     interest costs of the August 14 financial package, and for possibly\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     higher than budgeted interest payments on bonds for FIDF. Expanded\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     government spending through the central government and the public\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     enterprises is being carefully designed to maximize the impact on the\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     real economy and on the social safety net.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    * Second, on monetary policy, money market interest rates have already\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     declined substantially, from over 20 percent earlier this year to the\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     10-12 percent range. As the effects of these reductions work\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     themselves through and financial institutions are recapitalized,\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     banks' lending rates should fall further, and liquidity conditions\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     improve. This process will be helped by the ongoing decline in\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     inflation.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    * Third, financial and corporate debt restructuring-crucial to the\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     timing and strength of the recovery-are being carried to a decisive\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     stage. Through a comprehensive announcement on August 14, remaining\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     weaknesses in the financial system have been addressed, further\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     consolidation achieved, and conditions established for the use of\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     public resources in recapitalizing banks and facilitating corporate\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     debt restructuring.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    * Fourth, the strategy for corporate debt restructuring includes, in\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     addition, the removal of institutional obstacles and tax\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     disincentives, and the elaboration of a comprehensive framework to\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     guide market-based debt workouts under the Corporate Debt\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     Restructuring Advisory Committee (CDRAC).\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    * Fifth, additional measures have been taken to cushion the impact of\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     the recession. We have augmented and better targeted the social safety\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     net, while avoiding entrenching new costly schemes that could\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     introduce distortions into the labour market. Infrastructure spending\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     is to be increased to support priority areas in rural and urban\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     sectors in order to augment broader agricultural production and\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     industrial restructuring. We will also accelerate the social\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     investment programme targeted at addressing many dimensions of\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     unemployment.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    * We are committed to further market opening of Thailand's economy. Many\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     sectors have been sheltered from foreign investment, and thus denied\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     both new liquidity flows as well as international expertise. In\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     response, the Alien Business Law will be converted into a new and more\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     liberal Foreign Investment Law, taking care to balance the need for\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     new foreign investment flows with domestic sensitivities.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par      Privatisation of Thailand's utilities is also to be launched. The\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     privatisation process will generate resources that will be used to\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     protect the situation of workers while also contributing to reducing\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     the public debt.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 4. We will continue to keep the financing of the programme under close\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par review to assure that, with IMF support, the programme remains fully financed\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par at all times.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 5. Significant progress has been made in rebuilding confidence and paving\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par the way for a sustainable and strong economic recovery. Were new pressures\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par to arise, the government stands ready to take, in consultation with the\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Fund, whatever additional measures may be necessary to ensure the continued\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par success of the programme.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab  Sincerely,\par \tab  Tarrin Nimmanahaeminda\tab \tab \tab      M. R. Chatu Mongol Sonakul\par \tab    Minister of Finance\tab \tab \tab \tab Governor, Bank of Thailand\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Attachments\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Mr. Michel Camdessus\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Managing Director\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par International Monetary Fund\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Washington, D.C. 20431\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \tab \tab      MEMORANDUM ON ECONOMIC POLICIES\par \tab \tab \tab OF THE ROYAL THAI GOVERNMENT\par \tab \tab \tab      August 25, 1998\par \tab \tab   I.  MACROECONOMIC FRAMEWORK AND POLICIES\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 1. Steady progress in implementing the programme has restored net\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par international reserves to comfortable levels and helped stabilise the baht.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Nevertheless, the economic recovery has been delayed, partly reflecting the\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par deepening recession in Japan and elsewhere in the region. Against this\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par background, the main focus of this review has been to address key\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par obstacles-corporate indebtedness and inadequate bank capitalisation-to\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par economic recovery, within a supportive macroeconomic policy framework.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 2.  While the government remains committed to the overall macroeconomic\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par framework developed at the last review, some modifications are necessary in\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par light of the developments in the first half of 1998. Key elements of the\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par modified framework are presented below.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \tab \tab  Table 1. Macroeconomic Framework, 1997-98\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    -------------------------------------------------------------------\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab    1997\tab    1998\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    -------------------------------------------------------------------\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab    (in percent)\par    Real GDP growth\tab \tab \tab \tab  -0.4\tab    -7.0\par    CPI inflation (end-period)\tab \tab \tab 7.7\tab    8.0\par    CPI inflation (period average)\tab \tab    5.6\tab    9.2\par \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab (In indicated units)\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    External current account balance\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par      (In billions of U.S. dollars)\tab \tab    -3\tab   11-12\par      (In percent of GDP)\tab \tab \tab     -2\tab     10\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    Gross official reserves (end-period)\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par      (In billions of U.S. dollars)\tab \tab    27\tab   26-28\par      (In months of imports)\tab \tab \tab    5\tab    7-7`\par      (In percent of short-term debt)\tab \tab  90\tab    155\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    -------------------------------------------------------------------\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 3. In recent months, manufacturing production (seasonally adjusted) has\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par stabilised, and the agricultural sector is beginning to respond to much\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par improved relative prices. Thus, the modified macroeconomic framework\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par improved relative prices. Thus, the modified macroeconomic framework\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par anticipates that the economy will bottom out toward the end of the\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par year, setting the stage for a modest recovery of growth in 1999. A\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par supportive regional environment will be important to this prospect.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Reflecting the deeper recession, we now expect inflation to be lower and\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par the external current account surplus higher than previously forecast.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Within the overall framework, fiscal policy will continue to be assigned\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par the task of offsetting the recessionary impulse from falling domestic\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par demand, while monetary policy will aim at maintaining broad exchange rate\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par stability and ensuring adequate liquidity in the economy.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Fiscal policy\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 4. The larger fiscal deficit target for 1997/98 (3 percent of GDP on a GFS\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par basis for the overall public sector, excluding the cost of financial sector\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par restructuring) agreed at the last review remains appropriate. It is our\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par intention to fully utilize the increased margin in the fiscal programme to\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par support domestic demand. The performance criterion on net bank credit to\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par the public sector (Annex A) has been modified, consistent with changes in\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par the composition of the financing of the overall public sector deficit.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 5. Given the continued weakness in domestic demand, we will defer any\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par contraction of the fiscal stance in 1998/99. Thus, we will aim at an\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par unchanged overall public sector deficit of 3 percent of GDP, of which the\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par central government component is 1 percent of GDP. This excludes 1` percent\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par of GDP in interest costs related to the ongoing fiscalization of bonds for\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par FIDF and other financial restructuring costs. Of these costs, 1 percent had\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par already been budgeted; an additional ` percent is earmarked for the initial\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par interest costs of the August 14 financial package and for possibly higher\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par than budgeted interest payments on bonds for FIDF. Within this framework,\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par the government will channel an additional B 12 billion for the Social\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Investment Programme (SIP) and expand the scope of the Social Security Fund\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (SSF), as part of the broader effort to strengthen the social safety net\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (paragraph 26).\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 6.  The deficit of the state enterprise sector is now projected to rise\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par from ` percent of GDP in 1997/98 to 2 percent of GDP in 1998/99. Some part\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par of the increased deficit is explained by lower earnings forecasts. However,\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par the larger state enterprise deficit target importantly also reflects a\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par strengthened and better targeted expenditure programme, the composition of\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par which has been discussed with World Bank staff. In particular,\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par infrastructure projects, aimed at alleviating bottlenecks and supporting\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par employment in both urban and rural areas, are part of the revised\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par expenditure programme of the state enterprises. They constitute an integral\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par part of the government's efforts to cushion the impact of the recession on\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par employment.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 7.  The 1998/99 budget framework contains a number of structural measures.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Most importantly, we have either eliminated or temporarily suspended taxes\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par that could hinder debt restructuring efforts and mergers and acquisitions,\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par whose costs in terms of foregone revenues are negligible (Section III). As\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par part of future tax reform, aimed at restoring fiscal strength and improving\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par the efficiency of the tax system, we are considering a package of measures\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par that would phase out tax exemptions, introduce capitalisation rules\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par limiting interest deductibility, and streamline VAT collection and refund\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par procedures. In the meantime, we have appointed a customs advisor to\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par implement a programme for institutional and procedural improvements in\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par customs administration.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 8.  Based on the August 14 comprehensive announcement on financial sector\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par restructuring (Attachment I), we are reassessing the ultimate fiscal costs\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par of such restructuring. Our preliminary estimates suggest that the interest\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par costs associated with all restructuring \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  could average 3-4\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par percent of GDP on an annual basis in the next few years, before declining\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par gradually. Of this amount, as indicated above, we will fiscalize 1` percent\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par of GDP of interest costs in 1998/99. The remainder will be phased into the\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par budget over the two fiscal years 1999/2000 and 2000/2001. These estimates\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par include a preliminary assessment of the possible access to public funds for\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par the recapitalization of financial institutions (paragraphs 17-18).\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Monetary and exchange rate policy\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 9.  The monetary policy framework under the programme, which has emphasised\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par broad exchange rate stability, has delivered important results. It has\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par helped stabilise the baht, given credibility to the BOT's willingness to\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par respond to exchange market pressures, and allowed financial sector\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par restructuring to be implemented without disturbing monetary stability. It\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par is now allowing for a very substantial reduction in short-term money market\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par rates (presently about 10-12 percent, compared with over 20 percent earlier\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par this year). This policy framework will be maintained. Beyond that, as\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par inflation declines, there will be increased focus on restoring liquidity\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par and bringing down the broader structure of bank lending and deposit rates.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 10.  Regarding the conduct of monetary policy, we have begun to give\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par greater prominence to interest rates with 1-3 month maturities in the\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par repurchase market. This is aimed at providing the market with a somewhat\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par longer-term benchmark than the overnight repurchase rate. However, were\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market pressures to arise, short-term interest rates of\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par all maturities, including the overnight repurchase rate, would be promptly\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par raised to counteract speculative pressures.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 11.  Within this framework, the BOT has prepared a monetary programme as an\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par additional guide for monetary policy, based on the latest outlook for\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par inflation and output. At this stage, the reserve money programme sets upper\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par limits (B 495 billion for reserve money for end-December 1998), rather than\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par precise targets, for reserve money and NDA of the BOT (Annex B). Over time,\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par as money demand stabilises, greater prominence will be given to\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par quantitative targeting, and the BOT has begun to strengthen the\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par institutional capacity for monetary analysis and control.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 12.  In the context of the recessionary environment, a major bottleneck to\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par renewed credit expansion has been the reluctance of inadequately\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par capitalised banks to lend to over-indebted corporations. It is for this\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par reason that the overall strategy now focuses on accelerating corporate and\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par financial sector restructuring (Sections II and III). Meanwhile, the\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par government has supported the targeted extension of credit to the export\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par sector through trade-financed facilities with the Export-Import Bank of\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Japan and the AsDB. With the cooperation of the World Bank and the AsDB, we\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par are assessing the capacity of the specialised financial institutions and\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par their regulatory framework, in order to support their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par External sector\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 13.  The 1998 balance of payments outlook remains broadly unchanged. While\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par adjustment of the external current account continues to be greater than\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par envisaged, developments in Japan and the region, as well as those in\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Russia, have added offsetting pressures, reducing export demand and making\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par the rollover rate of maturing obligations more volatile. Based on\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par developments in the first half of 1998, we now expect the current account\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par surplus in 1998 to reach $11-12 billion or about 10 percent of GDP. With\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par falling export prices offsetting relatively buoyant growth in export\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par volumes, dollar exports are projected to decline by about 3 percent this\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par year. Consequently, the shift in the current account is being driven by the\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par continuing compression of imports. The capital account is now expected to\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par record a larger deficit, despite higher foreign direct investment, on\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par account of more volatility in the debt rollover rate.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 14.  Based on these projections, the BOT's gross reserves are expected to\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par remain within the $26-28 billion range by end-December 1998, as anticipated\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par at the time of the last review. However, reflecting the revised phasing of\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par some official financing disbursements, the net international reserve floor\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par for end-December 1998 has been slightly modified (Annex C). Reserves will\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par continue to provide more than full coverage of short-term debt. We will\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par monitor closely future capital account developments, and were pressures to\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par intensify, we would take appropriate measures to ensure that the economic\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par programme remains adequately financed.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 15.  Regarding the 1999 outlook, the capital account deficit is likely to\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par narrow sharply, as the large stock of offshore forward and swap obligations\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par will have been settled by end-1998. The current account is expected to\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par record a surplus of about $9 billion or 7 percent of GDP in 1999, based on\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par continued strength in export volumes and a possible rebound in import\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par demand. On this basis, and assuming some recovery in the regional\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par situation, the BOT's reserves could rise further in 1999. This should\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par increase room for manoeuvre and facilitate the process of lowering external\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par debt that is already under way.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \tab \tab    II.  FINANCIAL SECTOR RESTRUCTURING\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 16.  A comprehensive announcement on financial sector restructuring was\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par made on August 14 (attached). This announcement focuses on a wide range of\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par immediate measures to resolve Thailand's banking crisis, stabilise banks'\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par deposit base, and resume lending. There are four major aspects of the\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par financial restructuring programme. First, the consolidation of the banks and\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par the finance companies has been accelerated through additional BOT\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and proposed mergers. Second, private investment and entry\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (domestic and foreign) into the banking system is to be encouraged through\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par the early sale of two \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  institutions, as well as the preparation\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par of other state banks for eventual privatisation. Third, public funds are to\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par be provided for recapitalizing all remaining financial institutions, with\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par appropriate safeguards and conditions, and linked to progress in corporate\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par debt restructuring. Fourth, a framework has been developed for the creation\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par of private asset management companies. A timetable for key implementation\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par targets and dates for this agenda is contained in Box A.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 17.  The priority in using public funds is to facilitate the restructuring\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par and consolidation of the core banking system while, at the same time,\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par balancing the need for safeguarding the economy from further weakening. The\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par banks are expected to use the public capital injections, with new private\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par capital, to increase their capital positions above the current minimum.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  This should provide banks with the room necessary for adequate\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par provisioning, new lending, and advancing the implementation of the year\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 2000 LCP standards. The new windows for the use of public resources involve\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par the provision of Tier 1 and Tier 2 capital, with the latter linked to\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par progress in restructuring corporate debt. We are confident that the newly\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par announced mechanisms for recapitalizing banks with public resources are\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par flexible enough to address the different financial needs of individual\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par banks, and provide sufficient incentives for banks to access these\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par facilities. The modalities envisaged minimise moral hazard concerns,\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par through the write-down of the old shareholders and the adoption of new\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par restructuring plans (involving changes in management) in all institutions\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par receiving public equity support.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 18.  We have also reaffirmed our commitment to on-going and longer-term\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par initiatives designed to ensure that financial sector problems do not arise\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par again in the future. These initiatives, which have been part of previous\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par letters of intent, are summarized in Box B.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 19.  As indicated in the last Memorandum of Economic Policies (May 26,\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 1998), the government has initiated a broad review of financial sector\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par legislation and regulation, as well as of the information systems of the\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par BOT. The review is being conducted with the help of a Steering Committee of\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par international experts on BOT restructuring, as well as a Working Group on\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Financial Sector Policies. The review should be completed by end-October\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 1998, after which we will finalise proposals for legal, regulatory, and\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par institutional reforms, including that of bank supervision, consistent with\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par international best practices.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \tab \tab     III.  CORPORATE DEBT RESTRUCTURING\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 20.  Corporate debt restructuring is closely intertwined with financial\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par restructuring. Both are essential to reestablish normal market relations,\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par allow the resumption of credit flows, and support a recovery of the private\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par sector. A key focus is to reduce the debt burden of the corporate sector\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par which has risen sharply. Thus, restructuring of corporate debt is a key\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par focus of the strategy, essential for Thailand to restore sustained growth\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par over the medium term.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 21.  The government is committed to facilitating this process through\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par legal, tax, and other institutional reforms. The recently established\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Corporate Debt Restructuring Advisory Committee (CDRAC) seeks to promote\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par market-based corporate debt restructuring with a view to supporting the\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par economy and employment. Toward this end, we have developed a detailed\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par framework for restructuring and, in addition, have sought to eliminate\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par impediments and strengthen the broader incentive framework for\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par restructuring. Details of this approach are summarized in Box C, and\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par include the elimination of tax disincentives for debt restructuring;\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par revised BOT regulations for loan classification and collateral appraisal in\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par line with best international practice; and an increased involvement of the\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par BOT to monitor and supervise the debt restructuring carried out by\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par financial institutions under its regulations. A detailed timetable for\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par moving ahead with actual restructuring has been recommended as part of the\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par agreed principles; however, in larger and more complex cases, the lead\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par institution or steering committee may set a different timetable. At\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par present, based on the financial institutions' reports of debt restructuring\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par to the BOT, approximately 800 debtors (with total indebtedness of over B\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 200 billion) have begun the process of debt restructuring. Based on the\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par restructuring cases proposed by its five member associations, the CDRAC is\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par targeting 200 restructuring cases for early resolution.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 22.  We recognise that the market-based strategy can only succeed if the\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par incentive structure drives both debtors and creditors to accelerated\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par negotiation and resolution. Thus, it remains crucial to strengthen\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par bankruptcy legislation and to reform judicial foreclosure proceedings and\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par secured lending legislation as much as possible. Our strategy in this area\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par is contained in Box D. Additional amendments to the bankruptcy law have\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par been approved by the Cabinet and are expected to be enacted by the current\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par session of Parliament (by October 31, 1998). Regarding foreclosure and the\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par enforcement of security rights, the Cabinet has approved amendments to the\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Code of Civil Procedure which should significantly accelerate judicial\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par proceedings applicable to the enforcement of mortgages. These amendments\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par are also expected to be enacted by Parliament by October 31, 1998. We are\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par keeping under review the need for additional modifications, particularly to\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par the current regime of security rights, seeking to broaden the range of\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par assets that could serve as collateral, and introduce a regime of\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par centralized registration. We will finalise the additional changes by\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par end-1998.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 23.  Incentives for financial institutions to accelerate debt restructuring\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par with their corporate clients have also been increased by the scheme to\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par provide Tier 2 capital, with appropriate safeguards, to financial\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par institutions undertaking such restructuring. (Details are contained in the\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par attached August 14 announcement.) Corporate restructuring will also be\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par facilitated by envisaged market opening policies which should increase the\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par role of foreign investment in the economy (summarized in paragraph 27\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par below).\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 24.  We will not hesitate to strengthen this strategy for corporate debt\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par restructuring if warranted by circumstances. In particular, in the event\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par that protracted deadlocks develop, the CDRAC may \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to aid\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par negotiations-in addition to its role of reviewing existing legislation and\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par regulations-with the aim of facilitating debt restructuring in Thailand.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Progress in corporate debt restructuring will be a key focus of the next\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par quarterly review under the programme.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \tab \tab \tab    IV.  SOCIAL SAFETY NET\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 25.  As recognised in previous letters of intent, the government has a\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par vital role to play in supporting the recovery of the real sector and, in\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par the meantime, minimising the social impact of the recession. With the depth\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par of the recession more severe and prolonged than previously anticipated, we\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par are renewing our efforts in this regard.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 26.  Development of an adequate social safety net to cushion the impact of\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par the recession on the most vulnerable, remains a key priority of the\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par government. Our strategy has been developed in consultation with major\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par nongovernmental organisations and labour leaders, and is being implemented\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par with the support of the World Bank (a social investment project loan was\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par approved on July 9), the Asian Development Bank (a social sector loan was\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par approved on March 12), and the Overseas Economic Cooperation Fund. Key\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par elements of the strengthened social safety net are contained in Box E. In\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par designing the social safety net, we have avoided introducing distortions\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par into Thailand's relatively free and well functioning labour market.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \tab \tab \tab V.  MARKET OPENING POLICIES\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 27.  The government recognises that market opening policies can make a\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par significant contribution to ending the recession, accelerating the economic\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par recovery, and restoring sustained medium-term growth. However, these\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par policies, which aim at increasing the role of the private sector in\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Thailand's economy, need to be implemented with great care, and based on an\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par overall social consensus. In particular, these policies need to be\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par justified by the contribution they will make to reducing liquidity\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par shortages in the economy, increasing efficiency, supporting employment, and\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par restoring output. On this basis, we have drawn up a framework for\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par privatisation of key sectors (Box F). In addition, we are encouraging new\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par inflows of foreign direct investment by converting the Alien Business Law\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par into a new and more liberal Foreign Investment Law, and by liberalising\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par land leasing and sales (Box G) of the LOI.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab ATTACHMENT I\par \tab \tab JOINT STATEMENT BY THE \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 MINISTRY OF FINANCE\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  AND\par \tab \tab \tab    THE BANK OF THAILAND\par \tab \tab \tab      August 14, 1998\par \tab \tab Financial Restructuring for Economic Recovery\par \tab \tab \tab      I. Introduction\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 1. Structural weaknesses in Thailand's financial system have been at the\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par heart of the current crisis. Over the past year, a succession of measures\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par have been introduced to address these weaknesses and restructure the\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par financial sector. These have included: (i) requiring all financial\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par institutions to raise capital according to MOU's with the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Thailand\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (BOT); (ii) BOT \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in all institutions that could not succeed in\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par raising new capital (six banks and 12 finance companies, including today's\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par actions) and closing 56 unviable finance companies last December; (iii)\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par gradually tightening loan classification and provisioning (LCP) standards\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par to increase recognition by all institutions of their true financial\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par condition, as well as to bring these standards in line with international\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par practice; (iv) liberalising the limit on foreign equity in financial\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par institutions; and (v) establishing institutions for the disposition of the\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par assets of the closed finance companies. These are only some of the measures\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par that we have taken in our efforts to restore and maintain confidence in the\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Thai financial system. Moreover, through them, we have consistently\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par demonstrated that financial restructuring, and the restoration of viability\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par to Thailand's financial system, is an essential precondition to economic\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par recovery.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 2. However, the deteriorating domestic and regional economic environment\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par has now become a key obstacle to the task of restructuring Thailand's\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par financial system. The deepening economic recession has caused asset quality\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par of all financial institutions to deteriorate further. While several private\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par banks, including the two largest, have been able to secure injections of\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par private capital, their success has not been widely shared. Sharply\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par declining credit quality and falling earnings, because of the economic\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par downturn, are now impeding efforts of all financial institutions to raise\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par capital. These difficulties have contributed to tight credit conditions and\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par further complicated the task of economic recovery.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 3. Therefore, comprehensive and forceful actions are needed to address all\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par the outstanding weaknesses in the financial sector. This is the objective\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par of today's announcement. The augmented restructuring strategy is focused on\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par the following elements: (i) the commitment of funds to assist in the\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par restructuring efforts of viable banks and finance companies; (ii)\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par incentives for accelerating corporate debt restructuring pari passu with\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par recapitalization; (iii) the efficient management of nonperforming assets;\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (iv) the exit, merger, or sale of nonviable banks and finance companies;\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (v) equitable loss-sharing arrangements, containment of public sector\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par costs, and the avoidance of moral hazard; (vi) the strengthening of\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par prudential supervision and the accelerated adoption of international best\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par practice; and (vii) \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  restructuring of state banks and their\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par preparation for eventual privatisation.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 4. Such a comprehensive approach will require a high-level Financial\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Restructuring Advisory Committee (FRAC) to oversee its implementation. This\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par body is being established and will advise the Minister of Finance and the\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Governor of BOT regarding all aspects of the strategy. The composition of\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par FRAC will be finalised next week.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \tab \tab \tab II. Capital Support Facilities\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 5.  Against the above background, and recognising that the continuing\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par uncertainties about the macroeconomic outlook impede injections of new\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par private capital, the government has designed two capital support\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par facilities. Technical details of these facilities are contained in Annexes\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 1 and 2. The objective is to restore and maintain the solvency and\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par credibility of the Thai financial system and, most importantly, to enable\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par the financial institutions to perform effectively their role in supporting\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par economic growth.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 6.  The government recognises fully that the terms and conditions of any\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par public support facility should be transparent and subject to the strongest\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par possible safeguards, while facilitating the restructuring and consolidation\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par of financial institutions and protecting the economy from further weakness.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 7.  With the above aims in mind, we have developed two schemes to provide\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par funds to assist with the recapitalization of the viable financial\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par institutions (henceforth, "institutions" will refer to banks and finance\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par companies incorporated in Thailand), and to resume normal but prudent\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par lending. One scheme is aimed at catalyzing entry of private capital. The\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par other scheme provides financial resources and incentives to accelerate\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par corporate debt restructuring and encourage new lending. Both schemes build\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par in appropriate safeguards.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Tier 1 capital support facility\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 8.  Objectives: Under this scheme, the government essentially helps to\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par recapitalize the good assets of the institutions. As a first step, they\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par will be required to adopt up-front the end-2000 LCP rules. Although this\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par will bring forward provisioning requirements, it will remove uncertainties\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par associated with bad loans, and provide the basis for resumption of normal\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par lending practices.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 9.  Safeguards: This facility includes the following safeguards: First, the\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par costs associated with bringing forward the end-2000 LCP rules will be borne\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par by existing shareholders. Second, the new injections of public and private\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par capital will have preferred status over existing shareholders, thus\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ensuring that the existing shareholders will be the first to bear losses on\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par the old portfolio. Third, the government or the new investor will have the\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par right to change existing management. Fourth, resources will be made\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par available to these institutions provided their restructuring plans have\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par been approved by the BOT.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 10.  Capital injections: The capital injections under this facility will\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par take the form of the placement of tradeable government bonds, and subject\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par to the following conditions: (i) after full provisioning and up-front\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par write-off , if the Tier 1 capital adequacy ratio (CAR) falls below 2 `\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par percent, the government would be prepared to recapitalize the institution\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par up to this level; (ii) beyond this level, the government would inject Tier\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 1 capital up to the amount provided by private investors. We will be\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par supportive of institutions' efforts to recapitalize above the regulatory\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par minima, thus providing them with room necessary for adequate provisioning\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par and new lending.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Tier 2 capital support facility\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 11.  Objectives: This facility provides additional incentives to facilitate\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par corporate recovery through the provision of Tier 2 capital.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 12.  Safeguards: The safeguards include the requirement that eligibility is\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par dependent upon a legally binding debt restructuring agreement between the\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par financial institution and the debtor. In addition, the institutions will\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par need to satisfy the BOT that the debtor has been able to service the loan\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par according to BOT regulations on corporate debt restructuring (which require\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par that borrowers meet their obligations for three consecutive payment periods\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par or at least three months). The BOT will regularly conduct ex-post audits of\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par the restructuring agreements.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 13.  Capital injections: Capital injections under this facility will take\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par the form of the exchange of nontradeable government bonds for bank's\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par debentures, subject to the following terms and conditions: (i) the total\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par amount eligible for Tier 2 capital injection would comprise write-offs, net\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par of amounts provisioned, as well as 20 percent of the net increase in\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par outstanding loans to the private sector; (ii) while each institution would\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par be eligible to receive a maximum amount equal to 2 percent of risk weighted\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par assets, total Tier 2 support for increase in net lending will be capped at\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 1 percent of risk weighted assets; (iii) no single debt restructuring\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par agreement would be eligible for more than 10 percent of the amount\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par available to the institution; and (iv) as an additional incentive to\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par stimulate early corporate debt restructuring and to increase net lending,\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par the injection of Tier 2 capital will be graduated. Since drawdowns under\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par this facility would be in the form of Tier 2 capital, based on commercial\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par terms, this facility would not impose a burden on public finances.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 14.  Additional incentives: Financial institutions which adopt up-front the\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par end-2000 LCP rules will be allowed to phase write-offs for debt\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par restructuring over a five year period, hence providing some additional,\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par temporary relief to capitalising banks.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \tab \tab \tab   III. Regulatory Changes\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 15.  In order to allow institutions to use this facility, the BOT will\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par issue a new regulation regarding the composition of the capital base in\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par line with recommendations under the Basle Concordat. The overall CAR ratio\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par will remain 8.5 percent for banks (slightly above Basle standards) and 8\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par percent for finance companies; however, the Tier 2 component will be\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par allowed to increase up to 50 percent of the capital base, in line with\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Basle standards. Moreover, the one percent provisioning for performing\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par loans will also qualify for Tier 2 capital, also in line with Basle\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par standards.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \tab     IV. Facilitating the Establishment of Private AMCs\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 16.  We recognise that additional modalities need to be made available to\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par financial institutions to provide maximum flexibility to deal with their\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par bad assets, while allowing institutions to restructure their balance\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par sheets. Thus, we are encouraging institutions to set up, capitalise, and\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par fund private Asset Management Companies (AMCs). The AMCs will be defined as\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par financial institutions to allow them to borrow (excluding deposits) and\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par re-lend funds (only to existing customers); and allow them full flexibility\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par in setting their interest rates. Thus, the government will propose\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par amendments to necessary legislation. Institutions will be able to transfer\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par assets to AMCs without legal impediments. All taxes and fees incurred in\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par transferring assets to the AMCs will be exempt. AMCs will be fully\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par consolidated into the institutions' balance sheet provided they own more\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par than 50 percent of the shares. BOT will shortly issue regulations regarding\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of AMCs.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \tab \tab  V. Resolution of the Four \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Banks\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 17.  The four \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  banks are severely undercapitalized and have a\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par large proportion of nonperforming loans. Given the potential costs to the\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par state, we believe that any resolution strategy for the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  banks\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par must adhere to the following key principles:\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    * Be cost efficient\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    * Facilitate consolidation of the banking sector\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    * Allow for new domestic and foreign investment\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    * Be credible and transparent\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    * Enhance confidence in the whole banking system\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    * Be consistent with the capital support facilities\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par With the above objectives, and with the assistance of an international\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par investment bank, we have developed the following resolution strategies:.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 18.  Bangkok Metropolitan Bank (BMB) and Siam City Bank (SCIB): It is\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par proposed to privatise these banks. In preparations for this, the end-2000\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par LCP rules will be brought forward and the banks recapitalized through a\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par debt to equity conversion of outstanding FIDF lending. The new investors\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par could bid for up to 100 percent of the entire bank, and absorb all assets\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par and liabilities, including the nonperforming assets. However, the\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par government will be prepared to provide a loss-sharing or stop-loss\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par guarantee on nonperforming assets, as well as a guaranteed annual yield to\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par service the liabilities. Potential investors will have to specify in their\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par bids the type of stop-loss and yield guarantee they require. It is expected\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par that the privatisation process will be completed and the banks taken over\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par by new investors before end 1998.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 19.  First Bangkok City Bank (FBCB): FBCB will be absorbed by KTB under the\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par same loss sharing arrangement as the privatisation of the above two\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par mentioned banks. The integration of FBCB with KTB has been proposed because\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par FBCB's branch network complements that of KTB. KTB will take over the staff\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par and all the FBCB branches and will have the right to later rationalise\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par staffing and the branch network.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 20.  Bangkok Bank of Commerce (BBC): All performing assets and liabilities\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par of BBC will be transferred to KTB. The staff, branches and nonperforming\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par assets will remain in BBC, which will be turned into a special financial\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par institution owned by FIDF. FIDF will determine the process of winding down\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par BBC (by end-1999) with the aim: (i) to maximize loan recoveries; (ii) to\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par rationalise the staff; and (iii) to wind down and sell off branches.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \tab \tab    VI. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Restructuring of KTB\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 21.  While KTB will play a key role in the consolidation of FBCB and BBC,\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par the government recognises that KTB also needs to be restructured and\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par recapitalized, with the ultimate intention of preparing KTB for\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par privatisation. With this objective, KTB will implement a comprehensive\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par restructuring plan. A new KTB Board will be appointed on end-August 1998,\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par with all but one member of the Board from outside the public sector. KTB\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par will also establish a new full-time Executive Board. A comprehensive plan\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par for the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  restructuring of KTB will be developed by outside\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par experts by end-November 1998. This plan has to be approved by the BOT and\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par the MOF before KTB is capitalised according to the end-2000 LCP rules.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \tab   VII. Resolution Strategy for the Two Banks and 5 Finance\par \tab \tab \tab  Companies \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Today\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 22.  To ensure that public resources would not be available to unviable\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par institutions, the BOT has today \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in two weak banks and 5 unviable\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par finance companies. These institutions were deemed unviable according to BOT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par standards and had failed to raise new capital. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  had become\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par necessary to protect the public interest. BOT has ordered these\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par institutions to write down capital and to reduce their value per share to\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par one satang.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 23.  We have developed a resolution strategy for all of the newly\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  institutions, leading to further consolidation of the financial\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par sector. Union Bank (UBB) and the newly \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  finance companies will be\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par managed and eventually integrated with KTT. Laem Thong Bank (LTB) will be\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par integrated with Radhanasin Bank. While this will further raise the share of\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par the state in the banking system, it remains our intention to seek a\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par strategic investor for Radhanasin Bank and, as indicated above, to prepare\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par KTB and KTT for privatisation.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \tab    VIII. Financing the Costs of Financial Restructuring\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 24.  Thus far, the burden of supporting financial restructuring has fallen\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par on the FIDF. Some of the FIDF exposure will be converted into equity as\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par part of the recapitalization of the restructured financial institutions.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  The government has already declared that it is prepared to take full\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par financial responsibility for the losses of the FIDF, by converting them\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par into government debt. This has been a key plank of Thailand's overall\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par economic programme supported by the IMF and other members of the\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par international community. It is also common to the experiences of virtually\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par all other countries that have been faced with systemic crisis in their\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par financial system. This conversion has already begun with the issuance of\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par government bonds in recent months.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 25.  The ultimate costs of financial restructuring will be offset, to some\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par extent, by asset recovery, dividend and interest payments from\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par institutions, privatisation receipts and, ultimately, by the recovery of\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par the economy. Nevertheless, it is recognised that there could be substantial\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par remaining costs which will be borne by the government. As a first step\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par toward meeting these costs, the government has already agreed to issue B\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 500 billion of government bonds. Additional losses will be fiscalized by\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par the government over the next two years.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 26.  Also in consultation with the IMF, we have assessed the implications\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par of these costs for the public debt burden over the medium term. Thailand's\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par public debt has been relatively low by international standards. At this\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par stage, it is impossible to precisely estimate the additional costs of the\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par financial restructuring. After incorporating possible ranges for the total\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par costs of financial restructuring, Thailand's central government domestic\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par debt will rise substantially in the next two years, while leaving total\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par public indebtedness below that of many other developing and middle-income\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par countries. As such, it should be sustainable, given its present low level,\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par and the anticipated contribution of future privatisation receipts as well\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par as asset recoveries.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par      IX. Enabling Legal Changes and Key Benchmarks for Implementation\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 27.  The following enabling legal amendments will be proposed to\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par parliament:\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    * Authorisation to issue up to B 300 billion of government bonds for the\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     Tier 1 and Tier 2 capital support schemes. In addition, when\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     additional losses to FIDF are identified, the government will stand\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     ready to fiscalize them by seeking authority to issue more bonds.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     * Amendment of the Commercial Banking Law to enable establishment of\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     AMCs.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     * Amendment of the Commercial Banking Law to facilitate the merger of\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     the banks; and the necessary changes in the associated tax and SET\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     regulations.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 28.  Key benchmarks for the implementation of today's announcement are\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par provided in the attached table.1\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -----------------------\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 1. This table is included as Box A of the LOI.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (C) Reuters Limited 1998.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021216du8q01ker\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
