\par\par \b \uc2  ASIA MARKETS SUMMARY - Markets generally easier. \b0\par\par\uc2 606 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Juni 1998\par 11:57\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  0950 GMT - Asian regional markets showed a generally easier bias on Thursday even though the dollar/yen remained below 139 ahead of next week's expected G7 deputies meeting. \par \par \uc2  ECONOMIC WOES PREDOMINATE \par \par \uc2  Traders are continuing to fret about future poor economic data after Malaysia, South Korea and Hong Kong posted negative first-quarter GDP and are starting to talk about an economic recession across much of Asia, including Japan. \par \par \uc2  The generally accepted definition of recession is two quarters of economic contraction. \par \par \uc2  THAI BAHT PUNISHED \par \par \uc2  The Thai baht fared badly losing nearly two percent at one stage from late Wednesday levels despite some tough talking by Thai officials. \par \par \uc2  Thai Finance Minister Tarrin Nimmanahaeminda said the government was ready to further tighten monetary policy if the baht came under more pressure from regional tension. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief Chatu Mongol said the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  was prepared to use interest rates and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to defend the baht and said an exchange rate of 40-45 baht to the dollar would not severely aggravate Thai economic problems. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the baht was being depressed by dollar demand from local corporates for loan payments and some buying ahead of Thailand's planned global bond issue. \par \par \uc2  But Thai stocks gained in lacklustre trade on buying of bank and finance issues for short-term speculation, brokers said. \par \par \uc2  RUPIAH STUCK, JAKARTA STOCKS FIRM \par \par \uc2  The rupiah was glued to the 11,500 per dollar level and failed to get a lift from news that Indonesia and its creditor banks had reached agreement on the major issues of a rescheduling of its private sector debt. \par \par \uc2  Deutsche Bank, which hosted the debt talks in Frankfurt, said the main points of a framework accord had been agreed. \par \par \uc2  It said the deal regulated the foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  denominated liabilities of Indonesia's interbank sector and corporate sector and also included an agreement to maintain trade finance. \par \par \uc2  While a rollover of the crushing $80 billion corporate debt is crucial to jump-starting the economy and restoring investor confidence, dealers said the market was more focused on political and social concerns. \par \par \uc2  Jakarta shares jumped 3.5 percent on the rescheduling deal. \par \par \uc2  "Jakarta has not had good economic news for a long time," said Andre Cita, associate director of Bahana Securities in Jakarta. "This could be the start of a recovery for several companies." \par \par \uc2  RINGGIT AND KL STOCKS DOWN \par \par \uc2  The ringgit sagged through the 3.95 level to the dollar and shares gave back all of their morning gains, with the markets unimpressed by Finance Minister Anwar Ibrahim's announcement of plans for the newly formed Asset Management Company. \par \par \uc2  Anwar said the AMC would raise 10 billion ringgit through bonds to fund the purchase of bad loans from financial institutions. \par \par \uc2  Of the total amount, the equivalent of $1 to $2 billion ringgit would be raised from the international bond market through a government-guaranteed issue. \par \par \uc2  He said the AMC had not identified any projects for funding, but was expected to take over the first non-performing loans by the end of July. \par \par \uc2  HONG KONG STOCKS HIT HARD \par \par \uc2  Hong Kong stocks closed sharply lower as worries about a shrinking economy continued to gnaw at the market and brokers said a mood of caution reigned. \par \par \uc2  "The index is reflecting a bearish sentiment on the economy," said David Williamson, director at Indosuez W.I. Carr. \par \par \uc2  "The only thing that will boost it up is short-covering." \par \par \uc2  Gains made on Wednesday on the back of short-covering were erased on Thursday but brokers said derivatives-linked trading continued to set the tone. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du6403pfz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
