\par\par \b \uc2  ASIAN EXOTICS - Baht sags, rupiah cold to debt deal. \b0\par\par\uc2 725 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Juni 1998\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  0910 GMT - Asian regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  looked soft in late trade despite the dollar's inability to revisit 139 yen ahead of next week's expected G7 deputies meeting. \par \par \uc2  * The Thai baht fared the worst, losing nearly two percent from late Wednesday levels despite some tough talking by Thai officials. \par \par \uc2  * Thai Finance Minister Tarrin Nimmanahaeminda said the government was ready to further tighten monetary policy if the baht came under more pressure from regional tension. \par \par \uc2  * \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief Chatu Mongol said the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  was prepared to use interest rates and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to defend the baht. \par \par \uc2  * He also said an exchange rate of 40-45 baht to the dollar would not severely aggravate Thai economic problems. \par \par \uc2  * Referring to Thailand's move to float the baht last July, Chatu said: "We used to be a sitting duck but now we are a moving target". \par \par \uc2  * Dealers said the baht was being depressed by dollar demand from local corporates for loan payments and some buying ahead of Thailand's planned global bond issue. \par \par \uc2  * Tarrin said earlier this week Thailand was likely to go ahead with the first $1-2 billion tranche of a global bond issue in July, depending on market conditions at the time. \par \par \uc2  * Dealers said importers appeared to have hedged against a baht slide, but the baht was being hit by offshore sales, partly from funds getting out of the sagging stock market. \par \par \uc2  * The rupiah was glued to the 11,500 per dollar level and failed to get a lift from news that Indonesia and its creditor banks had reached agreement on the major issues of a rescheduling of its private sector debt. \par \par \uc2  * Deutsche Bank, which hosted the debt talks in Frankfurt, said the main points of a framework accord had been agreed. \par \par \uc2  * It said the deal regulated the foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  denominated liabilities of Indonesia's interbank sector and corporate sector and also included an agreement to maintain trade finance. \par \par \uc2  * While a rollover of the crushing $80 billion corporate debt is crucial to jump-starting the economy and restoring investor confidence, dealers said the market was more focused on political and social concerns. \par \par \uc2  * "The debt talk used to be very significant but now political concerns and how to make basic foods available at affordable prices are more significant," a foreign bank dealer in Jakarta said. \par \par \uc2  * About 3,000 protesters rallied outside Jakarta's parliament on Wednesday demanding that former Indonesian president Suharto be tried for corruption. \par \par \uc2  * The demonstrators also called on President Jusuf Habibie to resign. \par \par \uc2  * The ringgit sagged through the 3.95 level to the dollar, with the market largely unimpressed by Finance Minister Anwar Ibrahim's announcement of plans for the newly formed Asset Management Company. \par \par \uc2  * Anwar said the AMC would raise 10 billion ringgit through bonds to fund the purchase of bad loans from financial institutions. \par \par \uc2  * Of the total amount, the equivalent of $1 to $2 billion ringgit would be raised from the international bond market through a government-guaranteed issue. \par \par \uc2  * He said the AMC had not indentified any projects for funding, but was expected to take over the first non-performing loans by the end of July. \par \par \uc2  * Malaysia posted a 3.57 billion ringgit trade surplus in April, below the 3.72 billion surplus in March, but the figures were not seen as positive as imports shrank 8.5 percent, pointing to a contraction in the domestic economy. \par \par \uc2  * The Philippine peso shed its initial poise and weakened as companies bought dollars near the lows of 38 pesos. \par \par \uc2  * The troubled Taiwan dollar was unable to make much headway from the yen's gains as routine dollar demand from importers and the stock market's sharp drop thwarted its rebound. \par \par \uc2  * Taiwan's weighted index ended 2.84 percent lower at 7,425.96 points as Wall Street's losses and strong margin calls prompted investors to dump electronics shares. \par \par \uc2  * Finance Minister Paul Chiu attributed the market's losses to pychological factors and said Taiwan's economic fundamentals remained sound. \par \par \uc2  * He said the ministry had approved T$35.8 billion in local mutual funds in recent days. \par \par \uc2  * South Korean financial markets were closed as voting got underway in provincial elections, which were widely expected to see low turnout due to concerns about the country's economic problems. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du6403p29\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
