\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-New Thai c.bank chief seeks independence. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Vithoon Amorn \par \uc2 766 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Mai 1998\par 11:20\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BANGKOK, May 13 (Reuters) - Outspoken new Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Chatu Mongol Sonakul stressed on Wednesday the need for preserving the beleaguered institution's independence in his first official statement. \par \par \uc2  The new bank chief told a news conference he expected the economy to start improving from the third quarter, and that a stable baht at around 38 per dollar would aid Thailand's economic recovery and lead to lower interest rates. \par \par \uc2  Chatu Mongol said he would set up and chair a new nine-member advisory committee to screen the bank's interest rate, foreign reserves and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  exchange policies. \par \par \uc2  "The Bank of Thailand and the Governor should exercise their powers independently. They should be responsible for themselves and report occasionally to the finance minister," he said. \par \par \uc2  Chatu Mongol has in the past been a vocal critic of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which was blamed by a government-appointed panel for policy mistakes that led to the baht's sharp depreciation last year and the erosion of billions of dollars in foreign reserves. \par \par \uc2  The finance ministry is setting up a panel to assess whether some top former \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and ex-government officials could be prosecuted for making the mistakes which plunged Thailand into its worst economic crisis in decades. \par \par \uc2  Chatu Mongol replaced Chaiyawat Wibulswasdi, who quit last week after being named by a government panel as one of the officials responsible for policy mistakes. \par \par \uc2  The new governor is known to have close links with the ruling Democrat Party of premier Chuan Leekpai. Last week Chatu Mongol vowed to retain his independence in the face of market doubts. \par \par \uc2  The new governor pledged that authorities would maintain a policy of guaranteeing deposits to forestall public runs on institutions rumoured to be facing cash-flow problems. \par \par \uc2  He assured clients of private financial institutions that their deposits would be protected, and hoped a more stable baht would ease a severe liquidity crunch gripping Thailand and squeezing businesses. \par \par \uc2  At least two finance firms faced rumours of growing financial troubles this week after they failed to raise capital. \par \par \uc2  "The (government bailout) policy is essential or public deposits would all be withdrawn...with their deposits guaranteed, I see no justification to close financial institutions. Closure would complicate their future rehabilitation," Chatu Mongol said. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Financial Institutions Development Fund last year gave 430 billion baht in loans to debt-ridden finance companies, 56 of which were closed late last year, saddling the body with heavy debts. \par \par \uc2  The new governor said he would not encourage lower interest rates but said a stable baht, at around its current 38 per dollar level, could help speedy economic recovery. \par \par \uc2  "The current tight liquidity cannot be sustained. It does not hurt to see lower interest rates but I would not encourage it...a stable baht at 38 (per dollar) is probably a good rate to promote fast economic recovery as it would eventually lead to lower rates," he said. \par \par \uc2  The baht has rallied in recent weeks to the 38-40 range from a historic low of 56.90 in January. \par \par \uc2  The governor said he would place a higher priority on curbing inflation than on easing liquidity through lower interest rates. \par \par \uc2  Thai rates are among the highest in the region, with the minimum lending rate standing at 15.50 percent. High rates and cautious bank lending have aggravated the liquidity crunch. \par \par \uc2  Economist Banluesak Pussarangsi of the Mutual Fund Plc said lending rates could not ease in the next few months, given massive recapitalisation that Thai banks were ordered to undertake this year. \par \par \uc2  "It is hard to see rates coming down. The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  has limited tools and dwindling options to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , he said. \par \par \uc2  Chatu Mongol said the battered Thai economy would bottom out after the third quarter of 1998. \par \par \uc2  "I expect that from the third quarter things will start improving, provided that we can continue to hold the fort and if there are no unforeseen situations, like massive runs on deposits," he said. \par \par \uc2  Analysts see a bigger Thai economic contraction this year than a 3.0-3.5 percent decline forecast by government officials. \par \par \uc2  Chatu Mongol said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would ask weak banks and finance firms write down equity shares and help them raise capital to offset surging delinquent loans. \par \par \uc2  He voiced satisfaction over past \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  supervision of financial institutions but said regulatory enforcement would be beefed up and strict action taken against offenders. \par \par \uc2  He said the bank would launch a new litigation unit to act against offending financial institutions. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du5d05ktn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
