\par\par \b \uc2  Thai baht battered to record low for third day. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Rajan Moses \par \uc2 529 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Januar 1998\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BANGKOK, Jan 7 (Reuters) - The Thai baht was on Wednesday battered to another record low for the third straight day this week -- victim of pounding from the ongoing regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  turmoil. \par \par \uc2  In the onshore market, the savaged baht dived to an all-time low of 53.25 against the dollar in the day. It was quoted late at 53.00/53.20, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Offshore, the baht hit an all-time low of 54.50 earlier in the day but was quoted firmer late at 52.80/53.00 as the Malaysian ringgit and Indonesian rupiah, which sank to historic lows, recouped losses, they added. \par \par \uc2  The scramble for dollars against the baht was also capped late by fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Finance Minister Tarrin Nimmanahaeminda's statement that the government would announce measures on Friday to curb \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  speculation. \par \par \uc2  The baht has now plunged by over 50 percent against the dollar since its flotation last July. \par \par \uc2  "People were wary in the morning of Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and I think that will still keep the baht capped tomorrow. The target in the morning was 53.50 and we still haven't reached that," said a dealer at a local bank. \par \par \uc2  New regulations introduced on Wednesday to speed up dollar repatriation by exporters also limited losses. \par \par \uc2  "It could be another reason why any bid around 53.20 is being hit by offers," another dealer added. \par \par \uc2  Tarrin told reporters: "I have consulted the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor and will announce measures for curbing speculation against the baht on Friday." \par \par \uc2  Thailand, which is weathering its worst economic crisis, is saddled with a severe liquidity crunch, loss of foreign investor confidence and a sharply slowing economy. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers said they would wait for the Friday measures which, they speculated, may involve changes to regulations governing trading of commercial papers, especially those used by speculators to bet against the baht. \par \par \uc2  "It is very hard to predict what measures will come out. I think the government action would be partly influenced by the baht's performance today and tomorrow. If it keeps falling, we may see tough measures on Friday," a dealer at a foreign bank said. \par \par \uc2  The stock market trod water for most of the day and ended up a minuscule 0.04 point at 370.31 as turmoil in the baht raged around it. \par \par \uc2  Foreigners kept the market afloat by buying into export-oriented electronics and agricultural issues for long-term investment, brokers said. \par \par \uc2  Most investors, however, stayed on the sidelines worried about to what further depths the baht would plumb and negative news for the market from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  that three top commercial banks had agreed to recapitalise. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Chaiyawat Wibulswasdi said the three banks -- Bangkok Bank Plc, Thai Farmers Bank Plc and Bank of Ayudhya Plc -- would raise 30 billion baht ($569 million) in new capital. \par \par \uc2  This would be in addition to 70 billion baht of fresh equities to be issued by 12 other smaller banks by the end of the first quarter of this year, he added. ($1 = 52 baht) \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1998 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010926du17017qa\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
