\par\par \b \uc2  Thai baht tumbles to record lowcontagion, stocks s. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Anchalee Koetsawang \par \uc2 633 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Januar 1998\par 06:16\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BANGKOK, Jan 6 (Reuters) - Thailand's embattled baht tumbled to another record low on Tuesday hammered by a stampede for dollars against most regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers and analysts said. \par \par \uc2  But the stock market index, lingering now near nine-year-lows, stabilised at 367.44 at 0415 GMT, up 1.26 points after an early drop in thin, lacklustre trading. \par \par \uc2  The baht dived below 52.00 to the dollar early on Tuesday. It was quoted around 52.10/52.40 at 0330 GMT in the onshore market compared with 50.00/50.20 late on Monday. \par \par \uc2  Offshore, the baht plunged even further, to a day low of 54.40 versus 49.85/50.35 in the previous session. \par \par \uc2  To avert further falls in the baht, Finance Minister Tarrin Nimmanahaeminda said the cabinet would issue a statement to urge Thais and exporters with dollars to sell them for baht to help boost the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "Today at the cabinet meeting, I will propose the government issue a statement calling on people and exporters who have dollars to sell them into the market...This will help the baht," he told reporters before the start of the weekly meeting. \par \par \uc2  The finance ministry and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  were looking into additional measures to fend off \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  speculation, he said. \par \par \uc2  Dealers and analysts said the plunge in regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , especially the strong Singapore dollar and Malaysian ringgit, signalled a sustained, massive capital outflow from the troubled Asian region. \par \par \uc2  This would make it tougher for crisis-hit Thailand, which is weathering its worst economic crisis in decades, to tackle its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and other economic problems, they told Reuters. \par \par \uc2  The dollar's relentless march against the yen also made matters worse, they said. \par \par \uc2  "Regional factors drove the baht this low. The ringgit, rupiah, won were all hit. This is a disturbing sign, of money flowing out of the region, not just Thailand," said a dealer with a major European bank. \par \par \uc2  "Corporates rushed to buy dollars, fearing that if they did not cover positions now, their foreign debt burden could swell further," she said. \par \par \uc2  Traders suspected the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  but said any kind of defence would not provide much support for the baht if the sell-off in regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  continued. \par \par \uc2  The market was still digesting the news that the Thai government would seek to review the terms of its $17.2 multilateral bail-out package arranged for it by the International Monetary Fund, they added. \par \par \uc2  "It is a sensible move and the fact that IMF package is a flexible one which is reviewed every three months gives some hope that some of the conditions could be adjusted to suit the changing environment," said Satien Tantanasarit, vice president of Thai Danu Bank. \par \par \uc2  But others cautioned the move to review the package could backfire. \par \par \uc2  "It is also a delicate issue which could develop into a negative factor if not properly handled," said one dealer. \par \par \uc2  The dollar buying squeezed liquidity in the money market, sending the overnight interbank rate soaring to 25-26 percent among major local banks. But some foreign banks were said to be quoting rates as low as 17-18 percent. \par \par \uc2  While most investors stayed on the sidelines in the stock market because of the baht's steep fall, some bought cheap bank shares which helped the index post modest gains, brokers said. \par \par \uc2  "It's mixed. Some still look to buy counters that have minimal \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  exposure and those in the export sector which benefit from the weak baht," said one broker from a major local house. \par \par \uc2  "But other negative factors, the baht, the swelling debt, weighed on sentiment. Moreover, no one believes that the market has already bottomed out," he said. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1998 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010926du16015yk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
