\par\par \b \uc2  Thai baht in free fall, hit by Asia  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2  woes. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Vithoon Amorn \par \uc2 571 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Dezember 1997\par 13:14\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BANGKOK, Dec 12 (Reuters) - The Thai baht extended its free fall late on Friday tumbling to its all-time low of 45.60 to the dollar depressed by the battered Korean won and the Indonesian rupiah, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Spot baht onshore was quoted at an all-time low of 45.50/45.80 to the dollar late on Friday against a 44.30/44.60 range early and 43.50/43.60 on Thursday afternoon. \par \par \uc2  "It traded at 45.60 in the late afternoon. Anything can happen to the baht if the (Korean) won and (Indonesian) rupiah are still under attack next week. There's not much Thailand can do to stop it," said a money market dealer at a foreign bank. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said they could not detect any signs of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  to defend the baht. \par \par \uc2  Prime Minister Chuan Leekpai and Finance Minister Tarrin Nimmanahaeminda said the baht's big setback on Friday was influenced solely by offshore factors. \par \par \uc2  "The heavy impact from South Korea and Indonesia is being felt widely throughout the region. We must accept the fact that this is unavoidable," Chuan told reporters. \par \par \uc2  The rupiah fell to 5,255/5,325 to the dollar late on Friday from an opening of 4,640/4,680. \par \par \uc2  The won, facing heavy beating for the fifth day this week, recovered after active \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Korean government which sent the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to close Friday at 1,710 to the dollar against 1,719.8 on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Chuan appealed for public calm, saying his government was determined to stabilise the baht through consistent economic policies. \par \par \uc2  Tarrin pledged he would focus on adopting sound economic measures that would boost market confidence. He said short-term Thai interest rates may have to stay high temporarily to help slow the baht's slide. \par \par \uc2  He told reporters he regarded the baht's early stability as more vital and urgent than easing heavy financing burdens faced by Thai businesses. \par \par \uc2  "On interest rates, we will keep them temporarily high to help ease domestic problems. Stability of our exchange rate is more important," he said. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Chaiyawat Wibulswadsi said: "At a time like this, we are hard pressed to resist the trend but we will try to slow down any rapid slide of the baht. We should not be panicked by news reports." \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the Bank of Thailand, in a move to slow market flight to dollars, raised its one-day bond repurchase rate to 22.00 percent at the close from 20.75 at opening. \par \par \uc2  They said the baht would have little chance of escaping the fallout of the regional debacle despite recent signs of improvement in Thailand's economic fundamentals. \par \par \uc2  "Our economic indicators have started to improve slowly but under the current circumstances, they don't mean anything in terms of helping the baht," a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer said. \par \par \uc2  Thai stocks, languishing near their nine-year lows, remained weak on Friday in the wake of the regional crisis but managed to end the day with only mild losses, brokers said. \par \par \uc2  The SET Index closed down 3.12 points, or 0.84 percent, to 368.39 on moderate 3.82 billion baht turnover partly boosted by a second consecutive day of big lot transactions, brokers said. \par \par \uc2  -- e-mail: bangkok.newsroom@reuters.com reuters \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokume