\par\par \b \uc2  Thai markets await govt decision on finance firms. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Suthep Chawla \par \uc2 689 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Dezember 1997\par 07:38\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BANGKOK, Dec 7 (Reuters) - Thai financial markets are expected to steady this week as focus turns a government announcement expected on Monday on the fate of 58 suspended financial firms, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  Last week the battered stock market hovered near nine-year-lows while the baht plunged to an all-time low of 42.90/43.10 on December 3 on mainly regional concerns. \par \par \uc2  The markets recovered slightly toward the end of the week as South Korea struck a deal for a $57 billion bail-out package with the International Monetary Fund and the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the baht, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "Investors are waiting for a government decision on the fate of 58 suspended finance companies which will be announced on Monday," said Thanadech Mahapokai, first assistant vice president of Book Club Finance and Securities. \par \par \uc2  "They also want to know how foreign creditors of the suspended firms would get their money back," he told Reuters. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the government must find ways to solve the problems of the suspended firms' foreign loans estimated at $16 billion. \par \par \uc2  Monday's announcement by Finance Minister Tarrin Nimmanahaeminda set for 0130 GMT was expected to build confidence in Thailand's ability to handle its worst economic crisis in decades, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  If tough action was taken against the firms, it would underline the government's commitment toward restoring the tired Thai economy under a rehabilitation plan agreed with the IMF, which brokered for the country a $17.2 billion bail-out fund. \par \par \uc2  The IMF has said most of the debt-ridden firms -- seen as largely responsible for Thailand's crisis -- were insolvent and should be shut permanently. Its director-general Michel Camdessus said last Friday he believed Thailand would make a bold decision on the firms. \par \par \uc2  Analysts expect less than 10 of the 58 suspended firms to reopen. Total assets of the suspended firms are put by industry sources at 800 billion baht ($190.5 billion) while their loans outstanding amount to about half their assets. \par \par \uc2  Singapore-based financial consultancy I.D.E.A. said in a report that market expectations that only three of the suspended finance firms would be allowed to reopen had helped support the baht last week. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had lent the firms over 430 billion baht to tide them over during their liquidity problems. \par \par \uc2  The Financial Restructuring Agency (FRA), which has recommended action against the finance firms, will separate their good assets from bad ones after the key announcement. The Asset Management Corp (AMC) will manage bad assets. \par \par \uc2  The FRA and AMC were set up by decree. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the Thai stock index, which finished at 388.77 on Thursday, the last trading day of last week, would possibly make a more significant recovery in the latter part of the first quarter of 1998. \par \par \uc2  The upcoming holiday season coupled with Thailand's current slump would keep most investors sidelined from the market. \par \par \uc2  "The country's economy is expected to show signs of improvement in the second quarter of next year and I think it would be the best time for investment on the market during the first quarter," Thanadech said. \par \par \uc2  Overall stock prices are valued at less than 20 percent of prices posted in mid-1996, he added. \par \par \uc2  "Any recovery in the market will require solid convincing evidence of an economic turnaround with renewed monetary expansion accompanied by lower interest rates," said Barry Yates, head of regional research at Seamico Securities. \par \par \uc2  High interest rates have risen further recently with major banks raising key ledning rates by 50 basis points. The minimum lending rate now stands at 15.25 percent, choking businesses. \par \par \uc2  The baht has lost almost 40 percent of its value since it was floated on July 2, causing heavy \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  losses for many companies. \par \par \uc2  "Looking ahead, the underlying (baht) sentiment has not changed. Market trades are thin and confidence is weak. However, we may be able to enjoy mild relief so long as no major blow up occurs this month," I.D.E.A. said. \par \par \uc2  ($ = 41 baht) \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011003dtc701ez4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
