\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS- \highlight22\uc2 Central bank \highlight\uc2  lifts Thai baht from slump. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Rajan Moses \par \uc2 709 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Dezember 1997\par 11:43\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BANGKOK, Dec 3 (Reuters) - Thailand's embattled baht dived into uncharted territory on Wednesday, but the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  managed to pull the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  out of the doldrums by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Analysts, however, predicted the baht would tumble further in the near term with no bottom in sight as long as an Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and financial crisis spooked the region. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  helped the baht recover to be quoted at 42.58/42.68 per dollar in late afternoon against a record low earlier in the day of 42.90/43.10. The day's traded low was 42.95. \par \par \uc2  "I think in the afternoon the baht is stronger because the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market," said a dealer at a local bank. Other dealers said they also saw Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  prop up the beleaguered \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the Thai unit was driven to record lows by dollar buying from companies which need to meet year-end debt repayments and on worries over South Korea's financial crisis and the battered won \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Historic lows reached by the Malaysian ringgit and the Indonesian rupiah added to its woes. \par \par \uc2  Earlier on Wednesday, Thailand's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor appealed for calm after the baht's plunge. \par \par \uc2  Chaiyawat Wibulswasdi told a news conference the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was monitoring the sliding Thai unit closely and would use its limited resources prudently to stabilise the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Tarrin Nimmanahaeminda also tried to talk up the baht by saying its plunge was caused by regional fallout rather than local issues, and there was not much Thailand could do because of limited financial resources. \par \par \uc2  "It is more of a regional problem than local. Won and worries over regional economic crisis depressed the baht," he said. \par \par \uc2  The weak baht dampened sentiment in the stock market but the index closed with only a small loss of 0.76 points at 377.38 as bargain hunters returned to an oversold market, brokers said. \par \par \uc2  Dealers and analysts told Reuters they had expected the baht to break the 43.00 per dollar level on Wednesday and possibly head toward the 45.00-level in coming months if Asia's financial problems lingered. \par \par \uc2  The baht has been dogged by misfortune and has lost almost 40 percent of its value since it was floated in July when the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was at about 26.00 to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Thailand is struggling with its worst economic crisis in decades and has accepted a $17.2 billion multilateral bail-out package arranged by the International Monetary Fund (IMF). \par \par \uc2  The finance sector is in trouble with 58 debt-ridden firms suspended by the authorities and a final decision on their fate expected on December 7. Businesses are closing and unemployment is on the rise. \par \par \uc2  "The Thai baht has been lacking in depreciation recently alongside with other regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and today's weakness is a catch up," said Andy Tan, general manager overseeing regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  at Standard and Poor's-MMS Singapore. \par \par \uc2  "Sentiment was also affected by Prime Minister Chuan Leekpai's comments that the economy was in worse shape than he had imagined." \par \par \uc2  Chuan was quoted by the International Herald Tribune as saying in an interview that "in truth there are deep down problems that are much worse than what the people perceive". \par \par \uc2  Speaking to Reuters, Tan said: "We are in uncharted territory. The regional crisis is showing no signs of abating." \par \par \uc2  "A lot hinges on how the Korean situation turns out. As long as there are uncertainties, dollar-baht will continue to slide. \par \par \uc2  "No real bottom is seen for now, hopefully we will get new direction and sentiment will change in the new year," he said. \par \par \uc2  Satian Tantanasarit, vice president at the Thai Danu Bank's treasury department, said he was surprised the baht had fallen so sharply in the onshore market. \par \par \uc2  "We are all surprised by the fall and that the baht should behave like this in the onshore market. People are all just following...it should cap at 43.00 per dollar today and in three to four months, if things continue like now, we could hit 45.00," he said. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011003dtc301ies\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
