\par\par \b \uc2  Thai  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  Woes May Lead IMF To Revise Terms Of Aid Package \b0\par\par\uc2 991 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Oktober 1997\par 16:15\par \uc2  Dow Jones Online News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1997, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  BANGKOK -(Dow Jones)- As its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reaches new depths, Thailand could be forced to renegotiate the terms of its $17.2 billion loan agreement with the International Monetary Fund, economists said Friday. But the new terms may prove even more onerous for the country. \par \par \uc2  Three months after the IMF launched its biggest economic rescue program since the 1995 bailout of Mexico, many analysts say it has little to show for its efforts in Thailand. The baht reached a historic low Friday of 41.30 to the dollar and stock prices tested new eight-year lows. Still, Thai policymakers continue to dither over economic reform. \par \par \uc2  "People who used to think that the baht wouldn't go as low as 40 became very nervous and scrambled to sell the baht as a result," said one dealer at Thai Danu Bank PCL. "Many of those people, unfortunately, were Thai companies who have a lot of foreign debts to repay." \par \par \uc2  Thai corporations are dumping the baht for dollars on fears their debt obligations will grow larger as the baht continues to lose its value against the U.S. dollar. Thailand's private sector owes some $80 billion to foreign creditors, $37 billion of which is due in the next 12 months. \par \par \uc2  As the baht continued its free fall, the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but declined to specify. Its action, however, did little to help the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said. Any dollar selling in the next sessions will be a result of profit-taking by Thai exporters who earned their income mostly in the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Experts say the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's persistent decline suggests Thailand will eventually need to ask the IMF for additional cash to shore up its foreign reserves amid accelerating capital flight. The IMF, for its part, will have to revise performance targets based on what most economists agree are wildly optimistic economic-growth forecasts of 2.5% economic growth in 1997 and 3.5% in 1998. Many economists predict outright recession in 1998. \par \par \uc2  "Given such a weak baht, it seems the targets are going to be unachievable," said Maya Pinto, a regional economist with Independent Economic Analysis in Singapore. "The targets have to be made realistic. They need to be renegotiated." Many economists expect the IMF's growth targets to be lowered once the agency completes its first review of Thailand's economic performance Nov. 30. \par \par \uc2  The IMF by then is scheduled to provide the second tranche of credit to Thailand, and analysts say it may insist on stringent new policy measures in return for its largesse. It may ask for a second increase in Thailand's value-added tax, said Christa Marti, an economist with UBS Securities in Singapore. The tax was increased to 10% from 7.5% in August at the IMF's urging. \par \par \uc2  Thailand's financial slide has been even worse than economists expected, causing some to draw comparisons to the Mexican peso debacle. "Mexico, three months into its crisis, had its act together," said Bernhard Eschweiler, of J.P. Morgan Inc. in Singapore. Thailand's government, by contrast, has deepened the crisis by fumbling and backtracking on key economic-reform policies, many economists say. \par \par \uc2  Friday, the Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  released data indicating just how great the cost of delay has been. Manufacturing activity in August fell 5.1% after rising 3.8% year-on-year in July, marking the first contraction in six years. Net capital outflows in August alone totaled 144.9 billion baht, up from 53.1 billion baht in July. In the eight months to August, the Bank of Thailand said, outflows totaled 229.3 billion baht. \par \par \uc2  Amid the outflows, only frequent drawdowns on the $17.2 billion IMF credit line has kept the country's foreign reserves from shrinking, experts say. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had used up $5 billion of that credit line, including $600 million in October. Thailand's foreign reserves stood at $30.7 billion as of Oct. 15, up from $29.6 billion at the end of September. \par \par \uc2  The capital outflows reflect a fear among investors that the Thai government won't act decisively to fix the economy for several months, thus permitting the baht to slide further. Prime Minister Chavalit Yongchaiyudh, who revoked a cabinet decision to raise oil taxes this month rather than inflame his constituents, could face a general election in January. In the interim, the Thai government will be loath to make unpopular economic decisions, analysts say. \par \par \uc2  Under the terms of its agreement with the IMF, Thailand has to maintain foreign reserves of $23 billion in 1997 and $25 billion in 1998. It must keep inflation below 9.5% in 1997 and 5% in 1998, and maintain a budget surplus equal to 1.6% of gross domestic product in 1997 and 1% of GDP in 1998. \par \par \uc2  If the baht continues to decline, most economists said, most of those targets will become unattainable. "If it falls further, it will simply be disastrous," said Chalongphob Sussangkarn, a leading Thai economist who heads the Thailand Development Research Institute (TDRI). The country could reach the point of a "debt moratorium" in which the private sector is simply unable to repay its debts. Thailand, at that point, may have to rely on foreign reserves to to pay its bills. \par \par \uc2  Thai stock prices closed mixed Friday as investors bought selected shares on the back of relatively better trade figures released by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said. But concerns over the plunging value of the baht still overshadowed the market. \par \par \uc2  The Stock Exchange of Thailand index closed 2.12 points higher at 447.21, compared with Thursday's close of 445.09. At Friday's close, the SET index was 46% lower than 1996's year-end close of 831.57. \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 1997 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020011006dtav00zey\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
