\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-Thais not renegotiating IMF aid, baht dives. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Rajan Moses \par \uc2 663 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Oktober 1997\par 10:34\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BANGKOK, Oct 30 (Reuters) - Thailand said on Thursday that it was not seeking to renegotiate the $17.2-billion bailout package arranged for it by the International Monetary Fund (IMF) as the baht plunged to a record low of 39.95 per dollar. \par \par \uc2  "It is not our intention at all to renegotiate the package. We cannot talk about renegotiation. I reaffirm that we will stick to the letter of the agreement with the IMF," Deputy Finance Minister Surasak Nananukul told Reuters. \par \par \uc2  He was commenting on a report in the International Herald Tribune on Thursday that said Thailand was seeking to renegotiate with the IMF. \par \par \uc2  The newspaper said Thai officials wanted to ease strict budget constraints and extend Thailand's borrowing limits in renegotiations early next week. \par \par \uc2  The baht's plunge, as Thailand weathers its worst economic crisis in decades, forced the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, a senior \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  official said. \par \par \uc2  After the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the embattled baht stabilised somewhat in the onshore market, dealers said, noting the unit had plummeted on strong demand for dollars from corporates to pay foreign debts or repatriate profits. \par \par \uc2  Surasak said an IMF team was in Bangkok to review the progress of Thai government steps to meet conditions in the IMF bailout package, but not for renegotiations. \par \par \uc2  He added that the IMF team was satisfied so far with the government's moves to cut its budget, and that Thailand had appointed a new head for its key Financial Restructuring Agency to revive, merge or liquidate 58 suspended Thai finance firms. \par \par \uc2  Major troubles in Thailand's debt-ridden finance and property sectors forced the country to take the IMF package in August to steady its tottering economy. \par \par \uc2  The government has taken painful measures, such as slashing 159 billion baht ($4.0 billion) in planned spending from its 1997/98 (October-September) budget and raising taxes on some imported goods, to meet IMF austerity requirements. \par \par \uc2  Separately, another deputy finance minister Chaturon Chaisang told reporters that his ministry proposed a further 18 billion baht cut in the budget. \par \par \uc2  The plan, to be discussed by the cabinet next Tuesday, would be an effort to try to offset revenue shortfalls that would result from recent government withdrawal of a controversial oil excise tax hike earlier this month, he said. \par \par \uc2  The government scrapped the unpopular one baht per litre excise tax increase on oil, which was a part of IMF austerity measures, after a major public outcry. \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Kosit Panpiemras told reporters on Thursday that civil servants need not fear their salaries would be cut as part of austerity measures. But cuts would be made in spending by state enterprises. \par \par \uc2  "There will be no oil tax increase and no salary cuts for civil servants. But there will be some cutting in the administrations of state enterprises," he said. \par \par \uc2  The IMF is also requiring Thailand to come up with a cash surplus of one percent of gross domestic product, equivalent to 69.8 billion baht, in its 1997/98 fiscal year. \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Kosit Panpiemras said that while Thailand would be able to meet the one-percent requirement, he was unsure if the numerical value of 69.8 billion baht could be met as the level of GDP growth was now uncertain. \par \par \uc2  To uplift the country's ailing export sector, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Thursday that it had raised the value of export credits to 33 billion baht from 26 billion baht. \par \par \uc2  The private sector would also chip in another 33 billion baht to uplift the sector which has been stung by a major liquidity crunch and a slowdown in exports growth, assistant governor Kiartisak Meechareon said. \par \par \uc2  Kosit said on Wednesday that Thailand proposed to cap its foreign debt at $4.0 billion, of which $2.0 billion could be used to boost liquidity in the exports sector. \par \par \uc2  ($1= 39 baht) \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011003dtau03ita\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
