\par\par \b \uc2  SE Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  -2: NT$ Hits 10-Yr Low \b0\par\par\uc2 337 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Oktober 1997\par 10:54\par \uc2  Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1997, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    The New Taiwan (NT) dollar ended at a new 10-year low\par against the U.S. dollar on overseas buying of the U.S.\par dollar through non-deliverable forward contracts, said\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  dealers.\par    The head of Treasury at an Asian bank's Hong Kong\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said he expects pressure on Taiwan's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par to devalue the NT dollar will continue and that it wouldn't\par surprise him to see "the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ...test the NT$31.00 level\par next week."\par    The U.S. dollar ended higher at NT$30.944, its highest\par level since July 30, 1987, from Wednesday's closing of\par NT$30.835, said dealers.\par    In \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Thailand\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  had to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market to stop the Thai baht's slide against the\par U.S. dollar earlier Thursday.\par    A \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  official said the Bank of Thailand is\par unconcerned about the baht's level, as the U.S. dollar hit a\par new all-time high of 39.95 baht against the Thai \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par    The dollar has eased slightly since mid-morning and is\par quoted at 39.90 baht at 0924 GMT (4:24 a.m.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  EST) Thursday.\par    The Hong Kong dollar was virtually steady at HK$7.7305\par late Thursday from the previous session, but traders warned\par that rising forward market premiums and interbank rates were\par the hallmarks of previous speculative moves against the Hong\par Kong dollar.\par    The treasury chief said a surge in the six-month forward\par premium to 3,000 points earlier in the day from 1,800 points\par late Wednesday, along with a rise in the one-month interbank\par rate to 15% from 10% late Wednesday could be just nervous\par investors looking to hedge Hong Kong dollar exposure in case\par traders in London or New York sell the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  short.\par    The U.S. dollar has climbed to 3.4325 Malaysian ringgit\par late Thursday, compared with 3.3945 late Wednesday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (END) DOW JONES NEWS  10-30-97\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 0454EST\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1136 4340 \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020011006dtau02c2s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
