\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Thailand \highlight\uc2 /Foreign Reserves -2: Minimizing Mkt  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 199 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Oktober 1997\par 11:00\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1997, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  BANGKOK (AP-Dow Jones)--\b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  expects its foreign reserves to rise above $30 billion after 1998, up from a target of $23 billion this year, Deputy Prime Minister Virabongsa Ramangkura said Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Virabongsa, the top Thai official negotiating Thailand's economic bailout by the International Monetary Fund, said at a press conference that reserves would grow slowly through 1998, rising to $24 billion in 1998. He said the targets had been set in consultation with the IMF. \par \par \uc2  Thailand's foreign reserves are at two-year lows in the aftermath of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's futile battle to defend the Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  against speculative attacks this year. The bank finally abandoned its fixed exchange rate system in July, causing the Thai baht to drop 30% against the dollar. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In its fight against speculators, the Bank of Thailand accumulated\par about $24 billion in swap-market obligations that come due within 10\par months. Virabongsa said the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has been careful recently to\par avoid \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the money markets, focusing instead on maintaining\par its foreign reserves.\par -By Warangkana Tempati; 662-266-0744; wtempati@ap.org\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (END) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 14-10-97\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 0900GMT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011006dtae07p89\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\p