\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-More pain as Asian  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2  plumb depths. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Phil Smith \par \uc2 687 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 September 1997\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Sept 3 (Reuter) - The agonisingly persistent weakening of Asian regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  continued with gusto on Wednesday with the Thai baht, Philippine peso and Malaysian ringgit all sagging to fresh record lows. \par \par \uc2  A lack of confidence in the Asian region as a whole in the wake of the Thai crisis continues to be the main driving factor and a perky U.S. dollar is not helping matters, analysts said on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The dramatic slide on most Asian stock markets reversed itself on the back of a record daily gain on Wall Street but this did nothing for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The baht lost seven percent of its value in the domestic market, sinking to 37.60 per dollar in the onshore market from a previous record low of 35.15 on Tuesday evening. \par \par \uc2  In Malaysia, the ringgit sank as low as 2.9860 to the dollar from 2.9360/410 at Tuesday's close while the peso touched 31.83 compared with the previous 30.89 close. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the action proves the slide in stocks and \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are not directly linked, although they have both recently been falling for similar reasons. \par \par \uc2  Wednesday's moves reflected a belief that administrations around the region were ready to allow \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to go even lower, they said. \par \par \uc2  "I think this reflects a realisation that the central banks will stop fighting so hard to defend the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said James Mitchell, a strategist at Salomon Brothers in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  Such a stance would allow monetary policy to be relaxed, something which would help stocks but obviously hinder the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  This was graphically displayed by the move in the Malaysian stock market, which slumped five percent while Thai and Philippine stocks rallied. \par \par \uc2  Malaysian Prime Minister Mahathir Mohamad appeared on television on Tuesday night vowing to continue to defend the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  He also announced a government proposal to spend 60 billion ringgit to buy back shares --- but said locals who wished to sell would get premium prices, while foreigners would get market prices. \par \par \uc2  Malaysian shares were the only ones to decline throughout the session although Thai stocks did give up early gains. \par \par \uc2  In the Philippines the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  actually cut its liquidity reserves for banks to six percent from eight percent on a staggered basis, an effective, albeit mild, monetary easing. \par \par \uc2  Thailand said was considering similar measures. Deputy Prime Minister Virabongsa Ramangkura said he had asked the Bank of Thailand to consider relaxing a rule that commercial banks and offshore banking units set aside seven percent of imported short-term foreign funds as reserves at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Ramangkura said he was also keen to see the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  shorten a maximum 180-day period that Thai exporters can keep their dollar or foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  denominated export earnings before converting them into baht. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, in Indonesia there was much talk that the recent sharp rise in \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  certificate rates, or SIBs, will be brought back down and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  was shrugged aside. \par \par \uc2  Even though the Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  actually \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market today, analysts do not believe the International Monetary Fund (IMF) -- which after providing its bail-out package does have some influence on policy -- will allow an aggressive defence of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Analysts also said there was little Asian governments and central banks could do to contain the sell-off in regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "The danger with \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  or very tough rhetoric is you increase the chance of market players trying to test the limits. I believe the best thing would be to let what's going on run its course," said Chan Chia Lin, head of economic research at ABN-Amro Bank. \par \par \uc2  While recent controls on non-trade \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  deals have stopped opportunist speculators from jumping on the bandwagon, \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are still vulnerable. \par \par \uc2  "The imposition of controls only potentially limits the amount of short-selling of your \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  by foreign players," Chan said. "But it doesn't protect you from demand for U.S. dollars from domestic corporates who have sizeable unhedged external exposure," she said. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011002dt93009i0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
