\par\par \b \uc2  S.E.Asian  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2  crumble as ringgit slumps. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Sonali Desai \par \uc2 750 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 August 1997\par 11:55\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Aug 28 (Reuter) - Southeast Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  crumbled on Thursday as the Malaysian ringgit threw off all remnants of poise and slumped to historic lows. \par \par \uc2  The Thai baht, the usually sturdy Singapore dollar, the Indonesian rupiah and the Philippine peso all followed the ringgit's descent. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the ringgit was hit by mounting worries about a possible trade deficit in July and investor panic triggered by the Kuala Lumpur Stock Exchange's unexpected move late on Wednesday to stop short-selling of major stocks. \par \par \uc2  The ringgit, which has now lost five percent in the past week, opened weak and ploughed through a series of historic lows, hitting 2.8920 to the dollar in late afternoon trade. \par \par \uc2  The Kuala Lumpur stock index fell 8.31 percent to a four-year low of 777.46 before recovering to close at 812.18. \par \par \uc2  "People are just liquidating their ringgit holdings. They don't want to play the market now that they've changed the rules," the head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at a Singapore bank said. \par \par \uc2  The ringgit was at 2.8840/90 to the dollar at 0930 GMT against 2.8230/80 late on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the market was now targetting the 2.90 level for the ringgit, though the stampede was slowed somewhat by fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Bank Negara. \par \par \uc2  "We think Negara might be up there near the 2.89 level. And on the swaps, every time we reach a certain level, they get pulled down," the Singapore dealer said. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  does not ordinarily comment on its market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  In \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the baht tumbled to a new low of 34.40 to the dollar in early trade on worries about domestic politics and declining foreign reserves. \par \par \uc2  Market interest was focused on a parliamentary economic debate seen as an effort to head of an opposition no-confidence motion and players showed little reaction to monthly economic data as it was braced for poor numbers. \par \par \uc2  Nor did Finance Minister Thanong Bidaya cause significant ripples by saying Thailand's forward swap contracts could deplete reserves by $6-14 billion over the next year. \par \par \uc2  "Thailand has already realised losses of $3 billion and for every day that the baht doesn't improve, they're going to lose a couple of hundred million dollars," a dealer said. \par \par \uc2  Thanong told parliament the expected foreign reserve loss would be covered by a $17.2 billion finance package arranged by the International Monetary Fund. \par \par \uc2  The baht was at 34.00/15 to the dollar onshore against 33.90/34.00 late on Wednesday. It was at 34.10/25 offshore. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar fell to a low of 1.5185 to the U.S. dollar, dragged down by the ringgit. It was at 1.5178/88 against 1.5030/40 on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The Indonesian rupiah headed back down towards the 3,000 to the dollar level, again largely because of the ringgit's fall. \par \par \uc2  "It's mainly the regional factor again. The ringgit was hit overnight in London and New York which drove the Singapore dollar lower and spilled over later to Indonesia," a U.S. bank dealer in Jakarta said. \par \par \uc2  The Philippine peso recovered from opening lows after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell dollars at the 30.30 peso level. \par \par \uc2  It was at 30.00/15 to the dollar against its opening 30.25. \par \par \uc2  The peso fell to a record low of 30.45 on Wednesday, partly on fears of funds fleeing the battered stock market. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  then sold $1 million and raised liquidity reserves by three percentage points. \par \par \uc2  The Manila share index lost 9.28 percent, its biggest drop in a decade, to end at 2,071.97 on Thursday following the release of lower first half economic growth figures. \par \par \uc2  The South Korean won was propped up by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's repeated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which triggered stop-loss dollar sales, lifting the won to 901.60 against Wednesday's close at 902.80. \par \par \uc2  The Taiwan dollar also ended firmer at T$28.688 as investors unloaded U.S. dollars in the face of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's stubborn defence of the T$28.727 level for more than two weeks. \par \par \uc2  After the market close, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  deputy governor Hsu Chia-tung said the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had no reason to fall further and the bank would hold firm in its defence of the T$28.727 level. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011002dt8s00icl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
