\par\par \b \uc2  Thailand has $23.4 bln fwd swap contracts-BOT. \b0\par\par\uc2 222 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 August 1997\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BANGKOK, Aug 21 (Reuter) - Thailand has $23.4 billion in forward swap contracts, which come due over the next 12 months, Bank of Thailand governor Chaiyawat Wibulswasdi said on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Chaiyawat said $8.6 billion of the contracts are onshore and $14.8 billion are offshore. \par \par \uc2  He said the onshore contracts would not have much impact on foreign reserves as they would be rolled over. He said if the offshore contracts were rolled over they would not affect foreign reserves. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  actively in defence of the baht when it was attacked by speculators in mid-May. Most three-month forward \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  contracts used by foreign hedge funds during the attacks either matured or were rolled over early this week. \par \par \uc2  Chaiyawat said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  took the $8.6 billion onshore forward swap contracts as part of its normal job of maintaining short-term domestic liquidity. \par \par \uc2  He said Thailand would only feel a gradual impact on its foreign reserves over the next 12 months when the $14.8 billion offshore forward contracts mature without being rolled over. \par \par \uc2  The governor said future lower Thai foreign reserves would be replenished by loans obtained from the IMF-sponsored package. \par \par \uc2  -- Bangkok newsroom (662)-6520642 \par \par \uc2  -- email: bangkok.newsroom@reuters.com \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011002dt8l00pgo\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
