\par\par \b \uc2  Thailand June Econ Data -2: Forward Positions Erode Reserves \b0\par\par\uc2 396 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Juli 1997\par 07:27\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1997, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Thailand's economic slowdown, however, is expected to have a beneficial effect on two indicators that once alarmed economists - the trade and current-account deficits. The consensus forecast calls for the current-account deficit to shrink to 21.38 billion baht in May from 38 billion baht in April. The trade deficit is expected to have shrunk to 22.4 billion baht in May from 41 billion baht in April. \par \par \uc2  'The data will be what everybody expected to see,' said an economist at a European brokerage in Hong Kong, who asked not to be named. 'The economy is going to weaken further, and the reserves are going to contract. But the question is by how much.' Under the circumstances, he said, the Thai stock market isn't likely to be ruffled. The bad news, the economist said, 'is already priced into the market.' \par \par \uc2  Analysts attributed the sharp drop in foreign reserves in June to a 'snowball effect' that began in May with the worst speculative attack on the Thai baht in 14 years. That attack caused the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to spend $4 billion of its reserves to defend the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in May. \par \par \uc2  That drop in reserves, announced in late June, caused foreign investors in Thailand to panic, resulting in big outflows of capital and obligating the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to spend yet more of its reserves in a vain attempt to defend the baht peg - eventually abandoned July 2. \par \par \uc2  Since then, the baht has dropped about 18% based on onshore market quotes, with the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  seen \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  occasionally to maintain at least a modicum of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stability. \par \par \uc2  Arporn Chewakrengkai, chief economist at Deutsche Morgan Grenfell Securities (Thailand) Ltd., said the loss of reserves reflected a crisis of confidence among both domestic and foreign investors. 'That isn't anything the Bank of Thailand can control,' she said. \par \par \uc2  Economists expect Thailand's reserves to continue to drop for the rest of the year, because of the forward positions taken by the Bank of Thailand in May during its attempts to defend the baht. \par \par \uc2  The Union Bank of Switzerland predicts that reserves could drop to $15 billion by year end. By law, Thailand is required to maintain reserves of at least $8 billion to back up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  (MORE) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 30-07-97 \par \par \uc2  0527GMT \par \par \uc2  1139 55126 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011006dt7u05k98\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
